# States allowed to exceed the committed window budget, with the reason and the ceiling.
#
# Format:  <view>  <maximum allowed window height in pt>
#
# This is a RATCHET, read by scripts/window-fit-check.sh. A state listed here is already known to
# miss the budget; the number is how badly it is allowed to miss it. Raising a number to make the
# gate pass defeats the only thing the file does. Removing a line when the state starts fitting is
# the intended end state.
#
# ## Empty since 2026-08-20, and that is the intended end state
#
# `content-starting` was listed here at 661 against a 620 pt budget. Both numbers are gone, for two
# separate reasons on the same day:
#
#   * the **instrument was wrong**. The gate read SwiftUI's declared minimum, which was 58 pt short
#     in every state because `WindowMetrics.deviceListFloor` asks the drive list for 46 pt and the
#     AppKit table backing it will not lay out below about 104. `starting` was really 718, not 661.
#   * the **duplicate refusals were collapsed**, which is the fix this entry said was worth ~80 pt
#     and deferred. It was: `starting` went 718 -> 638. In the four transient states the run state
#     is itself the reason every control refuses, and the status line already says so, so one
#     sentence draws instead of three.
#
# With the committed budget at 700 (user decision, 2026-08-20) every state fits with at least 62 pt
# to spare, so nothing needs an allowance. **Adding a line here is a decision, not a fix** — the
# format is `<view> <maximum allowed window height in pt>`, it is a ratchet, and raising a number
# to make the gate pass defeats the only thing the file does.
